The hook command `tasqx work` registers with the agent (no API call).

The agent runs it on its session events with the event's JSON on stdin (D248, D249). It acts only inside the `tasqx work` session it was registered for, named by TASQX_WORK_TASK and TASQX_WORK_SESSION (for codex, TASQX_WORK_SESSIONS), and never breaks the agent: anything it cannot do is a line on stderr, and it exits 0. You do not run it yourself.

Usage: tasqx hook [OPTIONS] <AGENT>

Arguments:
  <AGENT>
          The agent whose hook this is
          
          [possible values: claude, codex]

Options:
      --json
          Print the raw JSON API result instead of the human table

      --theme <THEME>
          Theme name (built-in nord|gruvbox|dracula|solarized|mono, or a user file). Overrides $TASQX_THEME and the config file (DESIGN.md §8)

      --socket <SOCKET>
          Local socket / named-pipe address of a tasqx daemon. Overrides $TASQX_SOCK. When a daemon is reachable, one-shot commands route through it (single writer); otherwise they run in-process. A set $TASQX_DB naming a different store than that daemon's is refused. Also selects the address for `daemon` and `watch`

      --no-daemon
          Never route through a daemon; always run one-shot in-process (the pre-daemon behaviour). Escape hatch for scripts

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

EXAMPLES
  tasqx hook claude    # what Claude Code runs on a session event
  tasqx hook codex    # what Codex runs on a session event

NOTE  `tasqx work --agent claude` registers this one command for the session's `SessionStart` and `SessionEnd` events through `--settings`, without touching `~/.claude` (D248). The command never varies; the event is read from the JSON the agent writes on stdin.
      Codex gets `tasqx hook codex` the same way from `tasqx work --agent codex`, through `-c hooks.*`, without touching `~/.codex` (D249). Codex runs a hook only once you trust it: run `/hooks` in codex once and trust `tasqx hook codex`. Until then codex skips it silently, and `tasqx work` finds the session from its rollout after codex exits, and says so once.
      It acts only inside the `tasqx work` session it was registered for: without `TASQX_WORK_TASK`, or for claude when the input's `session_id` is not `TASQX_WORK_SESSION`, it does nothing. Codex picks its own session id, so on `SessionStart` `tasqx hook codex` hands that id to `tasqx work`, which records it on the task when codex exits. Nothing else is recorded yet. A work session's tokens are measured from its transcript when the task completes, by a running daemon with `tasqx config set tokens.enabled true` (off by default); until such a daemon runs, the session stays unmeasured, and it is measured once one does, while its transcript is still on disk.
      It never breaks the agent: input it cannot read or an event it does not know is one line on stderr, and it exits 0. You do not run it yourself.

See also: work        Full manual: tasqx manual
